<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Surcouf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Surcouf was a French privateer and slave trader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> who operated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> from Île de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>. During the French Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> he captured many British merchant ships, most famously the East Indiaman Kent in 1800. He retired rich as a shipowner in Saint-Malo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-surcouf.jpg" alt="Robert Surcouf" width="300" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RTEmagicC_robert-surcouf_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
